    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-236735",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a51e317c-f6b3-5704-afb4-bdfc79f1176f",
      "knowledge_point": "Projecting a Vector Onto a Perpendicular Vector",
      "problem": "Let $p=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $q=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =6\\cdot x+1\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 6x-12 & =0 \\\\ 6x & =12 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
